---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="About me">
    <!-- Large photo across the full width -->
    <section class="hero">
        <img src="/images/anja-hero.jpg" alt="Anja Boin" />
    </section>

    <!-- Introduction -->
    <section class="intro content">
        <p class="label">About Anja</p>
        <h1>Welcome.</h1>
        <p class="lead">
            Airline pilot, mediator, software developer and above all an
            interested, curious human being. For 25&nbsp;years I flew around the
            world, yet my passion always lay with people: psychology and
            Nonviolent Communication. With my unusual career and experiences, I
            hope to inspire others.
        </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Main text -->
    <section class="content">
        <article class="story content">
            <h2>The curious girl in the corner</h2>
            <p class="first-paragraph">
                As a little girl, I was shy – and endlessly curious. My
                favourite place was a safe corner, from where I quietly watched
                the world go by. I devoured books, looked forward to the
                holidays all year long and dreamed of places I only knew from
                stories.
            </p>
            <p>
                School, on the other hand, was not my world. I often felt a
                little left out, and lessons couldn't keep up with my curiosity.
                When I had to choose a profession after secondary school, I
                faced a problem that would follow me all my life: everything
                interested me. Vet? But I didn't want to hurt animals. Surgeon?
                The long years of study put me off. Teacher? No way – even more
                school? After much hesitation, it became physiotherapy.
            </p>

            <h2>From the massage room to the cockpit</h2>
            <p>
                I enjoyed my studies. Practice "cured" me quickly: in a German
                massage practice I treated twelve patients an hour – every
                fifteen minutes a massage, a fango pack and an electrotherapy
                device. A conveyor belt instead of care.
            </p>
            <p>
                Then chance led me into the cockpit, and I knew at once: this
                was a workplace to my taste. Apart from my father, who would
                have loved to become a pilot himself, everyone around me thought
                I was completely unsuitable. After all, I was shy, had no feel
                for technology and not the slightest interest in aeroplanes. KLM
                also found me unsuitable in its selection process. I saw things
                differently – and was stubborn enough to become a pilot anyway.
                And that's exactly what I did.
            </p>
            <p>
                Whether that was really a good choice was debatable: I was
                afraid of flying. I just didn't know it yet. I spent some time
                in the United States to build up my flying hours, then obtained
                my licences in the Netherlands. My fear of flying didn't make it
                easy, especially when I was alone in the cockpit. But I have
                plenty of ambition and perseverance, and with an iron will I
                successfully completed my flight training at the NAV in
                Enschede.
            </p>

            <h2>Above the clouds</h2>
            <p>
                In 2001 my life in the air began, at Lufthansa CityLine in
                Düsseldorf: years full of flights criss-crossing Europe. I
                strolled through Paris, Milan, Bergen and Barcelona, discovered
                beautiful places and good food everywhere, and always lugged
                wine, cheese and pasta home from abroad. The foodie in me always
                came along – and usually had excess baggage.
            </p>
            <p>
                In 2015 I moved to the Airbus A340-300 as a Senior First
                Officer, and suddenly the world lay at my feet. The Maldives,
                Mauritius, Panama, San José – places the little girl in the
                corner had only read about in books. I went on excursions like
                crazy: I wandered through the botanical garden in Mauritius, lay
                on the snow-white beach of Siesta Key, visited temples in
                Chennai, swam with whales off Panama and snorkelled among sharks
                in the Maldives. Life as a pilot wasn't so bad after all. I
                enjoyed every minute. And every second.
            </p>
            <p>
                In 2019 the moment I had waited for so long finally came: my
                promotion to captain. Within six months I changed role, aircraft
                and routes – from two long-haul flights a month to twenty
                flights a week. That was tough. And it made me proud. Now I got
                to decide what needed to be done. At last I could really shape
                the atmosphere in the cockpit and make sure the whole crew was
                looked after. My caring side, which I had already developed as a
                First Officer, quickly came to the fore. And I brought some
                cheerfulness and lightness into the cockpit. Sometimes a bit too
                much. I didn't take life too seriously and above all wanted to
                have fun – and that's exactly what I tried to bring into the
                cockpit.
            </p>
            <p>
                Standing still was never my thing. At some point, flying no
                longer challenged me enough. I wanted to grow, discover new
                things and keep learning. Alongside flying I became a sommelier,
                and because the question of what moves people never let go of
                me, I trained in Nonviolent Communication from 2019 and became a
                mediator. Marshall Rosenberg had been a great inspiration to me
                for several years, and I wanted to follow in his footsteps, at
                least a little. When Covid grounded the aviation industry, I
                discovered various funding programmes. While other people were
                baking banana bread, I devoured courses – 52 of them in one
                year, all with a certificate. That much learning was enough for
                a few years. Oh yes, and I made water kefir too. To go with the
                banana bread.
            </p>

            <h2>A new chapter</h2>
            <p>
                In 2023, sudden and unexpected health problems brought my career
                as a pilot to a temporary end. All at once I was back on the
                ground – and had to wait. Still, I couldn't sit still. I started
                training as a driving examiner at the CBR, the Dutch driving
                test authority, but unfortunately couldn't complete it for
                health reasons. I applied for jobs as an energy consultant and
                as an IT specialist at KLM, but despite my many years of
                experience I couldn't find a position that satisfied me and also
                suited my health. So I started all over again: retraining as an
                application developer (Fachinformatikerin) in Oberhausen. My
                beloved dad was head of the IT department of a large government
                agency until his death in 1997 – and it's fun to now step into
                his beloved profession myself.
            </p>
            <p>
                Today I'm gathering up the threads of my life and weaving them
                together in a new way: the physiotherapist, the pilot, the
                mediator and the programmer. I'm looking for places where my
                curiosity has room to grow, where I'm challenged – and where I
                can have real, warm contact with people. In the cockpit I
                learned that a crew only flies well when everyone feels seen and
                heard. The same is true on the ground. I connect people, mediate
                in conflicts and listen – because truly listening is often more
                healing than you might think.
            </p>

            <p class="closing">
                The girl from the corner is still curious.
                <br />
                Only now she doesn't just watch the world.
                <br />
                She listens, she sees and she connects.
                <br />
                She helps shape it.
            </p>

            <div class="story-actions">
                <a href="/en/cv/" class="button">
                    My CV
                </a>
                <a href="/en/contact/" class="button">
                    Contact
                </a>
            </div>
        </article>
    </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
    /* Photo on a dark, warm background */
    .hero {
        display: flex;
        justify-content: center;
        padding: 3rem 1.25rem;
        background: var(--color-text);
    }

    .hero img {
        display: block;
        width: 100%;
        max-width: 65rem;
        height: auto;
        max-height: 80vh;
        object-fit: cover;
        border-radius: 0.5rem;
        box-shadow: 0 1rem 2rem rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.3);
    }

    /* Introduction centered and a bit wider */
    .intro {
        max-width: 52rem;
        padding-bottom: 0;
        text-align: center;
    }

    .label {
        display: inline-block;
        margin: 0;
        padding: 0.25rem 0.6rem;
        background: var(--color-text);
        color: var(--color-background);
        font-size: 0.8rem;
        font-weight: 600;
        letter-spacing: 0.08em;
        text-transform: uppercase;
    }

    .intro h1 {
        margin: 1rem 0;
        font-size: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4rem);
    }

    .lead {
        font-family: var(--font-headings);
        font-size: 1.4rem;
        line-height: 1.5;
    }

    /* Story: headings with a small accent line above */
    .story h2 {
        margin: 3.5rem 0 1rem;
        font-size: 1.8rem;
    }

    .story h2::before {
        content: "";
        display: block;
        width: 3rem;
        height: 3px;
        margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
        background: var(--color-accent);
    }

    /* Large first letter, like in a book */
    .first-paragraph::first-letter {
        float: left;
        padding-right: 0.5rem;
        font-family: var(--font-headings);
        font-size: 3.6rem;
        line-height: 0.9;
        color: var(--color-accent);
    }

    /* Closing sentence as a large quote */
    .closing {
        /* Wider than the text column, but never wider than the screen */
        position: relative;
        left: 50%;
        width: min(56rem, calc(100vw - 2.5rem));
        transform: translateX(-50%);
        margin: 4rem 0 3rem;
        padding: 2rem 0;
        border-top: 1px solid var(--color-soft);
        border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-soft);
        font-family: var(--font-headings);
        font-size: 1.6rem;
        font-style: italic;
        line-height: 1.4;
        text-align: center;
    }

    /* Buttons next to each other, centered */
    .story-actions {
        display: flex;
        flex-wrap: wrap;
        justify-content: center;
        gap: 1rem;
        margin-bottom: 2rem;
    }
</style>
